This version avoids recursion and performs inorder traversal with a stack. The stack gives the nodes in sorted order without storing the whole traversal. Whenever the current node is smaller than the previous node, I record the bad pair.

The first wrong node is set only once. The second wrong node is updated on every violation, which also handles the case where the swapped nodes are far apart in the inorder order.

```java
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null, right = null }
 * }
 **/

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

class Solution {
    void recoverTree(TreeNode root) {
        Deque<TreeNode> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        TreeNode current = root;
        TreeNode previous = null;
        TreeNode first = null;
        TreeNode second = null;

        while (current != null || !stack.isEmpty()) {
            while (current != null) {
                stack.push(current);
                current = current.left;
            }

            current = stack.pop();

            if (previous != null && previous.data > current.data) {
                if (first == null) {
                    first = previous;
                }
                second = current;
            }
            previous = current;
            current = current.right;
        }

        if (first != null && second != null) {
            int temp = first.data;
            first.data = second.data;
            second.data = temp;
        }
    }
}
```

An empty tree or a tree with one node simply finishes without a swap. The method changes node data in the original tree.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(h)` for the explicit stack.

The threaded traversal below is the C# version of Morris inorder traversal. It does not allocate a stack or a list. I keep `previous`, `first`, and `second` while the nodes appear in inorder order.

If no value is out of order, both target references stay null. That makes the code safe for an already valid tree as well as for a null root.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public void RecoverTree(TreeNode root) {
        TreeNode current = root;
        TreeNode previous = null;
        TreeNode first = null;
        TreeNode second = null;

        while (current != null) {
            if (current.left == null) {
                CheckNode(current, ref previous, ref first, ref second);
                current = current.right;
            } else {
                TreeNode predecessor = current.left;

                while (predecessor.right != null && predecessor.right != current) {
                    predecessor = predecessor.right;
                }

                if (predecessor.right == null) {
                    predecessor.right = current;
                    current = current.left;
                } else {
                    predecessor.right = null;
                    CheckNode(current, ref previous, ref first, ref second);
                    current = current.right;
                }
            }
        }

        if (first != null && second != null) {
            int value = first.data;
            first.data = second.data;
            second.data = value;
        }
    }

    private void CheckNode(
        TreeNode current,
        ref TreeNode previous,
        ref TreeNode first,
        ref TreeNode second) {

        if (previous != null && previous.data > current.data) {
            if (first == null) {
                first = previous;
            }
            second = current;
        }

        previous = current;
    }
}
```

Morris traversal briefly changes right pointers while it runs, but each temporary pointer is removed before that part of the tree is left. The input node values are modified at the end.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

The inorder order of a correct BST is sorted. While visiting nodes, I compare each value with the previous one. A drop means the two swapped nodes have been found. The first drop gives the first bad node, and the second drop updates the other one.

For example, an inorder sequence `1, 5, 3, 4, 2, 6` has drops at `5 > 3` and `4 > 2`. The nodes are `5` and `2`, so their data values are swapped.

```cpp
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *      TreeNode(int val) : data(val) , left(nullptr) , right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

class Solution {
    TreeNode* previous = nullptr;
    TreeNode* first = nullptr;
    TreeNode* second = nullptr;

    void inorder(TreeNode* node) {
        if (node == nullptr) {
            return;
        }

        inorder(node->left);

        if (previous != nullptr && previous->data > node->data) {
            if (first == nullptr) {
                first = previous;
            }
            second = node;
        }
        previous = node;

        inorder(node->right);
    }

public:
    void recoverTree(TreeNode* root) {
        inorder(root);

        if (first != nullptr && second != nullptr) {
            swap(first->data, second->data);
        }
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, since every node is visited once.
Space complexity: `O(h)` for the recursive call stack, where `h` is the tree height. The input tree is changed by swapping the two data values.

This one stores the inorder nodes and a sorted copy of their values. The inorder node list tells where each value belongs, while sorting the values gives the correct BST order. Then each node receives the value at the same position in the sorted list.

It uses more memory than the direct inversion check, but the steps are easy to inspect. For `4, 2, 3, 1`, sorting gives `1, 2, 3, 4`, so the four nodes are rewritten in that order.

```go
import "sort"

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * type TreeNode struct {
 *     Data int
 *     Left *TreeNode
 *     Right *TreeNode
 * }
 */

func recoverTree(root *TreeNode) {
    if root == nil {
        return
    }

    nodes := make([]*TreeNode, 0)
    values := make([]int, 0)

    var inorder func(*TreeNode)
    inorder = func(node *TreeNode) {
        if node == nil {
            return
        }

        inorder(node.Left)
        nodes = append(nodes, node)
        values = append(values, node.Data)
        inorder(node.Right)
    }

    inorder(root)
    sort.Ints(values)

    for i := range nodes {
        nodes[i].Data = values[i]
    }
}
```

The null-root case returns before creating any work. This changes the data stored in the nodes, not their child pointers.

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` because of sorting.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the node and value slices, plus `O(h)` recursion space.

